## FINDING 1 (T1) -- H-20260828-005 is REFUTED at the canonical ten gates, in BOTH directions

Registered `family_retail_overlap_reversal` and judged it through
`external_gauntlet.run_gauntlet` -- the existing door, no inserted threshold in either direction
(L1.60). Report: `desks/mt5/reports/gauntlet_H-20260828-005.json`.

24 preregistered cells, **all reported**: 3 symbols x rr{1.0,1.5} x ttl{2,4} x BOTH directions.
The card set `direction: 0` and said in terms that direction "is the gauntlet's question", so
publishing the fade arm alone would have been the producer answering a question it had deferred.
Charged trial census 31.

    survivors passing all ten:  0 / 24
    program level:              PBO 0.3295 (pass), Hansen SPA p = 0.827 (fail)
    deflated_sharpe:            fails 24/24, DSR ~ 0 against sr0 0.1142
    best cell of 24:            USDJPY follow rr=1.5 ttl=4, Sharpe 0.0297, EV 0.0249R
    fade arm alone (12 cells):  every Sharpe negative, -0.006 .. -0.164

The direction question is answered and the answer is NEITHER. Note the follow arm is *not* the
fade arm's mirror -- stops, targets and costs break the symmetry -- so "flip the sign of a
negative backtest" was checked rather than assumed, and it does not rescue the card.

**UNMEASURED, not failed (L1.28a):** the M15 arm the card prescribes. This box holds 203 `_H1`
parquets and no M15 for these symbols, so what ran is the H1 arm of the mechanism: a 2h hold is
2 bars instead of 8, and the entry is an hour late instead of a quarter. That residual is real
and is carded, not waved.

## FINDING 2 (T1) -- the card's clock LABEL was wrong and its INTEGERS were right, and the
## repair a reader would have made is the one that breaks it

The card says "15:00-17:00 UTC". Measured on the live parquets: **446 of 452 weeks in EURUSD_H1
begin Monday 00:00 and end Friday 23:00, in summer and winter alike.** No true-UTC FX tape can
do that -- its week boundary walks between 21:00 and 22:00 Sunday with DST. The stamps are
broker EET (UTC+3/+2) wearing a `+00:00` label.

The FX Blue corpus carries the SAME convention, and the tape proves it: the corpus trade-share
peak (15/16/17) IS the tape's own stamp-hour tick_volume peak -- EURUSD 7.3/7.8/8.5%, GBPUSD
7.0/7.6/8.2%, XAUUSD 6.7/8.4/8.4% -- while under a UTC reading the corpus peak would land on
18-20, where the tape falls away to 6.1/4.2/3.6%. So both clocks are broker EET, no conversion
is owed anywhere, and **anyone who had "fixed" the label by converting would have introduced the
three-hour error the label implied.** What is actually wrong is the mechanism narrative: stamp
15-17 is UTC 12-14, the London afternoon into the NY open, not the centre of the overlap.

## FINDING 3 (T1, repaired) -- `_h1` asserted a contract that is false, and gate 1 judges on it

`families._h1` said "Bars on this desk are broker-UTC by contract". They are not. The cost is
not arithmetic -- every family compares stamp-hours to stamp-hours and is self-consistent -- it
is a MECHANISM CLAIM, which is gate 1's entire subject. `family_london_close_momentum` fires at
stamp-hour 16 = 13:00 UTC in summer: it is named for the London close and trades the London
afternoon, and gate 1 passes it on the strength of that name. `family_usd_session_shock` runs
"London" 7-16 = 04:00-13:00 UTC, opening three hours before London does.

Corrected the contract in place, with the proof, and corrected the two mislabelled families.
**Hour constants deliberately NOT changed:** several were evidently chosen in broker time and
land correctly (comex `settle_hour=20` is 17:00 UTC, the real COMEX settlement), and silently
re-timing a tested cell is a worse defect than a wrong label. The re-timing question is carded.

## FINDING 4 (T1, repaired) -- the gauntlet's cell id collapsed 24 trials onto 8 names

`frontier_identity.cell_id` took its legacy short form whenever `rr` OR `wait_bars` was present
and dropped every other parameter. My 24 cells printed as 8 ids, three deep each -- **cells with
opposite-signed Sharpes under one name**, in the report that grants certificates. The
function's own docstring already said "arbitrary DSL parameters must never collapse onto
rr=?/wb=? IDs"; the branch predicate did not enforce what the docstring promised. The short form
now requires `{rr, wait_bars}` to be the WHOLE parameter set, so no historical id changes value.
This is the desk's dominant class again: a producer computes a distinction and the layer above
collapses it.

## FINDING 5 (T1, repaired + fenced) -- the orphaned-inode audit the 08-28 run owed

Owed item 4 was "apply the repair to every other long-running writer under desks/mt5/data". An
audit is a snapshot, so what landed is a fence: `scripts/check_orphanable_writers.py`, wired
07:41 daily in the manifest. The defect is a SHAPE -- an append handle held open across a loop
that waits -- and it recurs in whatever directory is written next.

Its first draft reported **58** hits and would have been unreadable. A loop flushing a list in
milliseconds is not exposed; narrowing to loops that WAIT (network/sleep/subprocess) cut it to
4, and restricting to git-TRACKED targets (an ignored artifact has no checkout to unlink it,
which cleared two retired crypto collectors writing to `data/*`) cut it to 2. **Both were real:**
the already-repaired fxblue miner, and one unfixed live instance --
`cohort_and_identity.observe_due` held its handle on the tracked
`desks/mt5/data/intelligence/cohorts/observations.jsonl` across a loop that sleeps 1s and waits
up to 20s per member, i.e. minutes, against a tree this box checks out every ten. Staged to /tmp
and published in one append. Fence green, its own test asserts the vulnerable shape, the
fast-loop false positive, the staged repair and the tracked filter.

